  "query_preview": "..Overall the forecast remains on track with only \nminor differences in placement of features and timing. Flash \nFlood Watch remains in effect until 11 PM Tuesday. \n\nAs for the MesoA...the fire hose of mid and upper level \nmoisture continues to stream into the region with a steady climb\nin SFC TDs. The only remaining layer of dry air is situated \nover portions of south-central AZ, roughly several hundred feet\noff the SFC, to 2-3k ft AGL. However, this is now rapidly \nmoistening as a weak SFC low is situated across SEAZ with \nincreasingly favorable upper level dynamics overspreading the \nregion. 500mb height falls are also evident, with cooling mid \nlevel temps to support an increase in elevated instability this \nevening into the overnight. This is leading to an overall upward\ntrend in coverage, with more lightning beginning to be evident.\nMUCAPE roughly 100-500 j/kg over much of SEAZ with pockets of \n500-1000 j/kg over portions of eastern AZ. With the better \ninstability and increasingly favorable upslope flow, we are \nseeing an increase in rainfall rates for the White Mtns. \n\nWidespread 1-2\" of rain remains likely area wide (exception of \nWestern Pima County 0.25-0.75\"), with a continued signal of \n2-4\"+ for the Sky Islands NE of Tucson and the White Mtns. In \nfact, some 75th/90th percentile REFS/RRFS guidance suggest 4\"+ \nis possible in the White Mtns, possibly extending SW into the \nSky Islands. Given the duration, possible training, and threat \nfor thunderstorms, this seems reasonable. Another thing of note, \nis the EFFI and Shift of Tales, which supports the potential for\nthese higher end totals. Over the past 24 hrs, this signal has \nonly grown. While some of the CAMs have been bouncing around a \nbit this morning and early afternoon, there are no changes in \nforcing mechanisms and forecast rainfall amounts from the \nEPS/GEFS/AIGFS/AIECMWF..etc. All of the guidance continue to \npaint robust rainfall totals across much of SEAZ.\n\nNow zooming into the Tucson Metro, generally 0.75-1.75\" is \nexpected for the event. The 75th/95th percentile (reasonable \nworst case) continues to paint totals of 2-3\"+. This includes \nthe 12Z HREF, showing similar amounts and continued 10%+ probs \nfor 3\" in a 6hr period Tuesday morning and/or afternoon. Timing \ncontinues to show a gradual increase in coverage this evening \ninto Tuesday early morning with peak potential Tuesday morning \ninto Tuesday afternoon. While these higher end totals are an \nincreasingly distinct possibility, it will favor upslope regions\nnear the Catalina/Rincon Mtns and/or repeated rounds (training)\nof heavier showers/storms. Another thing to consider is this \nwill not be your classic Monsoonal thunderstorms. It will be \ndeep tropical moisture with widespread showers and isolated to \nscattered thunderstorms, which may persist off and on for a \nlonger duration into Tuesday. With every event, there will be \nareas that under perform and over perform, dependent on where \nthese heavier bands of rainfall setup. WPC maintains a Slight \nRisk over much of the area for today and has a Moderate Risk for\nareas from Sells east, including Tucson, to the NM border on \nTuesday. \n\nOne side-note, severe storms will be possible Tuesday...but\nshould be considered a secondary threat compared to the risk of \nflash flooding. As the upper-level low tracks across the area,\ndecent forcing, marginal CAPE and abundant shear will be\npresent. Therefore, expect strong to perhaps isolated severe \nstorms...with brief transient low-top supercells also possible.\n\nOtherwise, lingering instability and moisture will be present \nWednesday for additional shower activity, but drier air will \nquickly lower precipitation chances through the day. A ridge of\nhigh pressure then builds into the Desert Southwest the second \nhalf of the work week, shutting down thunderstorm activity.",
